WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.266 --> 00:00:01.634 align:start size:94% position:6%
-Our next guest
is a best-selling author

00:00:01.634 --> 00:00:02.502 line:92%
and National Book Award winner

00:00:02.502 --> 00:00:05.271 align:start size:94% position:6%
His novel, "The Water Dancer,"
is available in paperback

00:00:05.271 --> 00:00:08.775 align:start size:94% position:6%
November 17th,
and the adaptation of his book

00:00:08.775 --> 00:00:09.576 line:92%
"Between the World and Me,

00:00:09.576 --> 00:00:12.145 align:start size:94% position:6%
airs November 21st
on HBO and HBO Max.

00:00:12.145 --> 00:00:15.315 align:start size:94% position:6%
Please welcome back to the sho
Ta-Nehisi Coates.

00:00:15.315 --> 00:00:16.549 size:84% position:42% line:92%
How are you, Ta-Nehisi?

00:00:16.549 --> 00:00:19.486 size:81% position:41%
-Hey! How's it going?
Nice to be here, Seth.

00:00:19.486 --> 00:00:20.887 line:92%
-It's always great to see you.

00:00:20.887 --> 00:00:24.157 align:start size:94% position:6%
For my money,
you've written some of the bes

00:00:24.157 --> 00:00:26.126 align:start size:94% position:6%
journalistic pieces
on the presidency,

00:00:26.126 --> 00:00:28.628 align:start size:94% position:6%
on the country,
over the last decade.

00:00:28.628 --> 00:00:33.133
You obviously have laid ou
your differences of opinio

00:00:33.133 --> 00:00:37.404 align:start size:94% position:6%
with Joe Biden over the years,
yet also made it very clea

00:00:37.404 --> 00:00:40.907
that he's a better choice if the
other choice is Donald Trump

00:00:40.907 --> 00:00:42.876 align:start size:94% position:6%
Based on that,
I'm assuming you were not

00:00:42.876 --> 00:00:44.511 align:start size:94% position:6%
celebrating in the streets
on Saturday,

00:00:44.511 --> 00:00:47.747 align:start size:94% position:6%
but how did you feel about
the people that were?

00:00:47.747 --> 00:00:51.117 align:start size:94% position:6%
-I wasn't. I was not
celebrating in the streets

00:00:51.117 --> 00:00:52.619 align:start size:94% position:6%
That's just not something
I would do.

00:00:52.619 --> 00:00:53.987 align:start size:94% position:6%
That's not how
I interact with the world.

00:00:53.987 --> 00:00:57.223 align:start size:94% position:6%
That actually is not even
any reflection on Joe Biden.

00:00:57.223 --> 00:00:58.792 align:start size:94% position:6%
I understand
the people who were.

00:00:58.792 --> 00:01:02.495
It's been a tough four years
I totally, totally get it.

00:01:02.495 --> 00:01:08.134 align:start size:94% position:6%
I do worry that
maybe we do not know how dar

00:01:08.134 --> 00:01:12.305
it's about to get, you know,
again, really, really quickly.

00:01:12.305 --> 00:01:15.508 align:start size:94% position:6%
But at the same time,
people are human beings.

00:01:15.508 --> 00:01:16.976 align:start size:94% position:6%
I understand it.
I get it.

00:01:16.976 --> 00:01:22.148
You know, Trump was a different,
you know, magnitude, you know,

00:01:22.148 --> 00:01:26.352 align:start size:94% position:6%
of corruption and a threat
to the state and to democracy.

00:01:26.352 --> 00:01:28.188 align:start size:94% position:6%
So I understand it.
I understand it.

00:01:28.188 --> 00:01:30.223 align:start size:94% position:6%
It wouldn't be what I would do
but I get it.

00:01:30.223 --> 00:01:33.460
-I want to talk about the sort
of two different sides of this

00:01:33.460 --> 00:01:36.229 align:start size:94% position:6%
that I think people
are weighing, which is, one --

00:01:36.229 --> 00:01:37.297 align:start size:94% position:6%
And you've said
about Donald Trump,

00:01:37.297 --> 00:01:39.299 align:start size:94% position:6%
you're not necessarily a racis
if you vote for him,

00:01:39.299 --> 00:01:42.302 align:start size:94% position:6%
but his racism
is not disqualifying for you

00:01:42.302 --> 00:01:43.336 align:start size:94% position:6%
if you vote for him.
-That's right.

00:01:43.336 --> 00:01:45.371 size:81% position:41%
-And he got more votes
than he did last time.

00:01:45.371 --> 00:01:47.507 align:start size:94% position:6%
On the other hand,
you have Joe Biden,

00:01:47.507 --> 00:01:51.311 align:start size:94% position:6%
you have Kamala Harris
got more votes than anyone

00:01:51.311 --> 00:01:58.051
So, how do you sort of see
the two working in concert

00:01:58.051 --> 00:02:01.121 align:start size:94% position:6%
-Um...Well, you know,
I-I don't know.

00:02:01.121 --> 00:02:04.691 align:start size:94% position:6%
It's distressing that
Donald Trump got more votes.

00:02:04.691 --> 00:02:08.261
I think we had just
a record level of participation,

00:02:08.261 --> 00:02:10.330
so the sheer amount of Americans
that were involved

00:02:10.330 --> 00:02:14.000 align:start size:94% position:6%
in the process was beyond
anything that we'd ever seen

00:02:14.000 --> 00:02:16.136 align:start size:94% position:6%
I think that's a good thing.
You know?

00:02:16.136 --> 00:02:19.873 align:start size:94% position:6%
I would prefer a world where
more people voted

00:02:19.873 --> 00:02:22.442 line:92%
and my side lost the argumen

00:02:22.442 --> 00:02:25.612 align:start size:94% position:6%
to a world where the other sid
worked to suppress

00:02:25.612 --> 00:02:30.049
the numbers of people who vote
and we lost because of that.

00:02:30.049 --> 00:02:33.153 align:start size:94% position:6%
Democracy -- you know,
you don't win every battle

00:02:33.153 --> 00:02:39.225
in a democracy, and that's fine,
so I was heartened by that

00:02:39.225 --> 00:02:41.494 align:start size:94% position:6%
I know how to deal
with losing the argument.

00:02:41.494 --> 00:02:43.463 align:start size:94% position:6%
You just go back.
You figure it out.

00:02:43.463 --> 00:02:45.331 align:start size:94% position:6%
You try to make
the argument again.

00:02:45.331 --> 00:02:48.501 align:start size:94% position:6%
So, you know, while on
some level it is depressing,

00:02:48.501 --> 00:02:51.938 align:start size:94% position:6%
and there's all sorts of thing
I can say about the appeal

00:02:51.938 --> 00:02:53.206 align:start size:94% position:6%
you know,
that Trump ultimately had

00:02:53.206 --> 00:02:54.974 align:start size:94% position:6%
and what that says
about the country.

00:02:54.974 --> 00:02:57.977 align:start size:94% position:6%
I think more people
participating is a good thing.

00:02:57.977 --> 00:02:59.245 line:92%
I'm not sure that, you know,

00:02:59.245 --> 00:03:02.582 align:start size:94% position:6%
the party in power right now
currently believes that.

00:03:02.582 --> 00:03:05.251
-I think it's safe to say
they don't believe that.

00:03:05.251 --> 00:03:06.386 align:start size:94% position:6%
-It's pretty safe.
Pretty safe.

00:03:06.386 --> 00:03:09.556
-Hey, I want to talk to you --
-I'm going out on a limb there

00:03:09.556 --> 00:03:10.757 align:start size:94% position:6% line:92%
[ Both laugh ]

00:03:10.757 --> 00:03:13.793 align:start size:94% position:6%
-I want to talk to you about
the fact that not just

00:03:13.793 --> 00:03:17.330
"Between the World and Me"
is about to be a TV show,

00:03:17.330 --> 00:03:20.767 align:start size:94% position:6%
but now "The Water Dancer"
is gonna be a film.

00:03:20.767 --> 00:03:24.070 size:84% position:42%
What is it like for you
to turn over your work

00:03:24.070 --> 00:03:28.975 align:start size:94% position:6%
to people that will then adapt
it for a different medium?

00:03:28.975 --> 00:03:32.045 align:start size:94% position:6%
-So, uh...difficult.
[ Laughs ]

00:03:32.045 --> 00:03:32.812 align:start size:94% position:6% line:92%
Very difficult.

00:03:32.812 --> 00:03:34.814
With "Between the World and Me,"
I had the great luxury

00:03:34.814 --> 00:03:37.217
of turning it over to
a very, very old friend of mine,

00:03:37.217 --> 00:03:41.454
Kamilah Forbes, who goes bac
to my days at Howard University.

00:03:41.454 --> 00:03:43.289 align:start size:94% position:6%
So that was pretty easy,
and she did a staging

00:03:43.289 --> 00:03:46.492
here in New York that I thought,
you know, went off pretty well

00:03:46.492 --> 00:03:48.795 line:92%
so I trust her implicitly.

00:03:48.795 --> 00:03:50.029 align:start size:94% position:6%
She's also
my wife's best friend,

00:03:50.029 --> 00:03:51.831 align:start size:94% position:6%
so that makes all of that
very, very easy...

00:03:51.831 --> 00:03:54.434 align:start size:94% position:6%
-Yeah, yeah, yeah.
-...to do.

00:03:54.434 --> 00:03:57.136 align:start size:94% position:6%
You know,
as for "The Water Dancer,"

00:03:57.136 --> 00:03:58.638 size:81% position:41%
I'm gonna be the one
writing the screenplay

00:03:58.638 --> 00:04:00.607 align:start size:94% position:6%
and doing the adaptation,
so that shows you

00:04:00.607 --> 00:04:03.676
about how much I trust people...
[ Both laugh ]

00:04:03.676 --> 00:04:06.079 align:start size:94% position:6%
...to adapt the work.
So, for good or for ill,

00:04:06.079 --> 00:04:08.648 align:start size:94% position:6%
you know,
we'll find out how that goes

00:04:08.648 --> 00:04:13.353
-You know, another thing you did
a lot of the ground work on,

00:04:13.353 --> 00:04:14.887 align:start size:94% position:6%
and we've talked about
this before,

00:04:14.887 --> 00:04:16.589 line:92%
but you had this incredible ru

00:04:16.589 --> 00:04:20.159 align:start size:94% position:6%
on the "Black Panther"
comic-book series.

00:04:20.159 --> 00:04:22.028 line:92%
This was before it was a film.

00:04:22.028 --> 00:04:25.632
And you also mentioned knowing
people from your Howard days

00:04:25.632 --> 00:04:29.802 align:start size:94% position:6%
You knew Chadwick Boseman
from your Howard days, as well

00:04:29.802 --> 00:04:32.171 align:start size:94% position:6%
What was that
whole journey like?

00:04:32.171 --> 00:04:35.808
-Uh...Well, you don't know
that you're on a journey,

00:04:35.808 --> 00:04:38.544 align:start size:94% position:6%
and so it's only
with his regrettable passing

00:04:38.544 --> 00:04:40.346 align:start size:94% position:6%
that, you know,
I've become wistful

00:04:40.346 --> 00:04:42.782 line:92%
and begun to think about it.

00:04:42.782 --> 00:04:47.353 align:start size:94% position:6%
You know, I met Chad,
wildly enough,

00:04:47.353 --> 00:04:50.323 align:start size:94% position:6%
when I was a student journalis
at the newspaper

00:04:50.323 --> 00:04:53.293 align:start size:94% position:6%
and he was leading
a protest at the university.

00:04:53.293 --> 00:04:55.228
And actually leading the protest
was Kamilah Forbes,

00:04:55.228 --> 00:04:57.163 align:start size:94% position:6%
who is adapting
"Between the World and Me,

00:04:57.163 --> 00:04:59.832 align:start size:94% position:6%
so it's the way that
these worlds come together

00:04:59.832 --> 00:05:01.301 align:start size:94% position:6%
And I was a journalist
at that time,

00:05:01.301 --> 00:05:04.203 align:start size:94% position:6%
so I was reporting on
what was a student takeover,

00:05:04.203 --> 00:05:08.308
and Chad was one of the leaders,
if not the leader.

00:05:08.308 --> 00:05:10.243 line:92%
So I knew him as, you know

00:05:10.243 --> 00:05:15.982
a student and a young man
of really strong principle

00:05:15.982 --> 00:05:17.850 line:92%
I also knew him as an artist

00:05:17.850 --> 00:05:20.920 align:start size:94% position:6%
not just as an actor,
but as a writer,

00:05:20.920 --> 00:05:22.588 line:92%
as a director at that time

00:05:22.588 --> 00:05:24.957 align:start size:94% position:6%
He is the last person
in the world

00:05:24.957 --> 00:05:28.995
that I would have thought
would become who he became

00:05:28.995 --> 00:05:31.831 align:start size:94% position:6%
not because of any lack
of talent, not because of any,

00:05:31.831 --> 00:05:36.736
you know, lack of ability or
intellect or anything like that.

00:05:36.736 --> 00:05:38.037 line:92%
But Chad was so principled, man.

00:05:38.037 --> 00:05:42.208 align:start size:94% position:6%
He wanted what he wanted,
and he wanted how he wanted it

00:05:42.208 --> 00:05:43.843 align:start size:94% position:6%
And, you know,
that was gonna be it.

00:05:43.843 --> 00:05:49.248
And so it's shocking to me t
watch what he ultimately became,

00:05:49.248 --> 00:05:53.653 align:start size:94% position:6%
and yet when he was chosen
to be Black Panther,

00:05:53.653 --> 00:05:55.788 align:start size:94% position:6%
I couldn't think of anybod
who would be better,

00:05:55.788 --> 00:05:57.090 align:start size:94% position:6%
because even back
in our Howard days,

00:05:57.090 --> 00:05:59.659 align:start size:94% position:6%
he had that same
sort of regal bearing

00:05:59.659 --> 00:06:02.295 line:92%
that he brought to T'Challa.

00:06:02.295 --> 00:06:06.032 size:84% position:42%
It wasn't a total reach
for Chad to do that.

00:06:06.032 --> 00:06:12.004 align:start size:94% position:6%
And it's a huge,
huge loss to see him gone.

00:06:12.004 --> 00:06:16.576 align:start size:94% position:6%
-You saw him, I guess,
it was probably at the premier

00:06:16.576 --> 00:06:17.243 align:start size:94% position:6% line:92%
of "Black Panther"?

00:06:17.243 --> 00:06:20.413 align:start size:94% position:6%
Is this the photo we have
of you guys together?

00:06:20.413 --> 00:06:22.548 align:start size:94% position:6%
-Oh, you guys blew that up
I did, yes.

00:06:22.548 --> 00:06:25.651 align:start size:94% position:6%
Yes, that's myself,
my wife, and Chad, yeah.

00:06:25.651 --> 00:06:28.521 align:start size:94% position:6%
-That's fantastic.
And it really is.

00:06:28.521 --> 00:06:31.391
What a journey to have bee
in college with somebody

00:06:31.391 --> 00:06:33.626 align:start size:94% position:6%
and then sort of help --
I don't know --

00:06:33.626 --> 00:06:35.294 align:start size:94% position:6%
rewrite the character
that made him so famous.

00:06:35.294 --> 00:06:38.164 align:start size:94% position:6%
-Yeah. That was --
It was such a coincidence, too

00:06:38.164 --> 00:06:40.933 size:75% position:38%
It was, like, weird.
Like I, you know...

00:06:40.933 --> 00:06:43.102 align:start size:94% position:6%
Like, from my perspective,
I, you know,

00:06:43.102 --> 00:06:44.904
wanted to be a comic-book writer
when I was a kid,

00:06:44.904 --> 00:06:47.006 align:start size:94% position:6%
but I never thought
it was actually gonna happen

00:06:47.006 --> 00:06:50.777 align:start size:94% position:6%
And I know the strongest par
of Chad was, you know,

00:06:50.777 --> 00:06:52.245 line:92%
the writing and directing part

00:06:52.245 --> 00:06:55.581 align:start size:94% position:6%
Here he becomes
this huge leading man.

00:06:55.581 --> 00:06:57.150 align:start size:94% position:6%
You know, the night
that picture was taken,

00:06:57.150 --> 00:07:00.119 size:88% position:44%
it was at the premiere,
and I saw him, you know,

00:07:00.119 --> 00:07:03.923 align:start size:94% position:6%
across the room.
And he, you know, had this -

00:07:03.923 --> 00:07:05.525 align:start size:94% position:6%
He never --
He never changed.

00:07:05.525 --> 00:07:06.259 line:92%
The celebrity part of him

00:07:06.259 --> 00:07:08.861 align:start size:94% position:6%
actually never changed
who he was.

00:07:08.861 --> 00:07:14.133 align:start size:94% position:6%
And he looked...
like he did not enjoy

00:07:14.133 --> 00:07:15.601 size:84% position:42% line:92%
being in a giant party,

00:07:15.601 --> 00:07:18.971 align:start size:94% position:6%
surrounded by a bunch of peopl
he didn't know.

00:07:18.971 --> 00:07:23.209 align:start size:94% position:6%
And my thinking was, you know,
having had about .001%

00:07:23.209 --> 00:07:24.844 align:start size:94% position:6%
of a taste of that,
I'm not even gonna go over

00:07:24.844 --> 00:07:27.180 align:start size:94% position:6%
and talk to Chad.
I'll see Chad when I see him

00:07:27.180 --> 00:07:29.816 align:start size:94% position:6%
My wife was so excited,
went over and talked to him,

00:07:29.816 --> 00:07:32.218 align:start size:94% position:6%
and he came over
and wanted to take this pictur

00:07:32.218 --> 00:07:32.885 size:88% position:44% line:92%
and talk and everything.

00:07:32.885 --> 00:07:37.590
You know, he was always just s
good about that sort of thing.

00:07:37.590 --> 00:07:40.927 align:start size:94% position:6%
I obviously didn't know
at the time that he was sick

00:07:40.927 --> 00:07:46.632 align:start size:94% position:6%
-That part of it is so tragi
and so retroactively --

00:07:46.632 --> 00:07:47.800 line:92%
retrospectively, I should say,

00:07:47.800 --> 00:07:50.002 align:start size:94% position:6%
so impressive
what he was going through.

00:07:50.002 --> 00:07:51.204 line:92%
-Yeah, no, he carried it.

00:07:51.204 --> 00:07:53.406
You know, and whatever pai
that he was going through,

00:07:53.406 --> 00:07:56.642 align:start size:94% position:6%
which I assume
to be quite considerable,

00:07:56.642 --> 00:07:58.544 align:start size:94% position:6%
he carried it.
-Well, thank you

00:07:58.544 --> 00:08:00.146 align:start size:94% position:6%
for sharing those stories
about Chad,

00:08:00.146 --> 00:08:01.314 align:start size:94% position:6%
and thank you so much
for being here.

00:08:01.314 --> 00:08:04.083
It's always such a pleasur
to talk to you, Ta-Nehisi.

00:08:04.083 --> 00:08:07.220 size:81% position:41%
-Thanks so much, Seth.
Thanks for having me.

